{"id":"1232347322261991425","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232347322261991425","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"@abhish_eksharma @PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela And greater similarity between the two corpora should make it easier for unsupervised seq2seq methods to align the two distributions","reply_to":"EthanJPerez","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225170633","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225170633/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232347322261991425","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1232346935958237184","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232346935958237184","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"@abhish_eksharma @PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela Thanks! Decomposition works even when using a random single-hop Q corpus. Using pseudo-decompositions is still better, perhaps since the pseudo-decomposition corpus is more similar to the multi-hop Q corpus than random Qs are (in terms of topic, entities, \"wh-\" word, etc.)","reply_to":"abhish_eksharma","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225172846","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225172846/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232346935958237184","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1232135322546274304","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232135322546274304","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"@PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela Overall, we find that it is possible to successfully decompose questions without any supervision and that doing so improves QA. (n/n)","reply_to":"EthanJPerez","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225041409","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225041409/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232135322546274304","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1232135206024273921","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232135206024273921","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"@PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela Qualitative examples show how question decomposition adds a form of interpretability to black-box QA models. In our analysis, we explore how sub-questions improve multi-hop QA, and we find that each component of our pipeline contributes to QA performance. (7/n)","reply_to":"EthanJPerez","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225041805","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225041805/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232135206024273921","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1232135106875101185","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232135106875101185","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"@PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela Our approach automatically learns to generate useful decompositions for all 4 question types in HotpotQA, highlighting the general nature of our approach. (6/n)","reply_to":"EthanJPerez","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225043425","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225043425/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232135106875101185","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1232135031239270400","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232135031239270400","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"@PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela QA models that use decompositions improve accuracy over a strong baseline on the original HotpotQA (+3.1 F1), the adversarial version (+11 F1), and the out-of-domain version (+10 F1). Our method is competitive with state-of-the-art methods which leverage strong supervision. (5/n)","reply_to":"EthanJPerez","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225041732","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225041732/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232135031239270400","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1232134918542483456","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232134918542483456","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"@PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela Step 3: We then give each sub-question and its answer as additional input to a multi-hop QA model. (4/n)","reply_to":"EthanJPerez","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225053155","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225053155/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232134918542483456","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1232134767086198784","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232134767086198784","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"@PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela Step 2: We use an off-the-shelf, single-hop QA model to answer decomposed sub-questions. (3/n)","reply_to":"EthanJPerez","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225070000","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225070000/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232134767086198784","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1232133571176849408","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232133571176849408","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"@PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela I still can't believe this works (n+1/n)","reply_to":"EthanJPerez","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225042517","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225042517/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232133571176849408","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1232129719471968256","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232129719471968256","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"@PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela Step 1: We learn to map from the distribution of hard Qs to that of simpler Qs with Unsupervised Seq2Seq methods. For training data, we construct a noisy, \"pseudo-decomposition\" for each hard question by retrieving similar, simple questions from a large corpus (&gt;10M Qs). (2/n) https://t.co/BfBKsoxTDI","reply_to":"EthanJPerez","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225075202","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225075202/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232129719471968256","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
{"id":"1232127027961942018","date":"2020-02-25","url":"https://x.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232127027961942018","author":"EthanJPerez","text":"New! \"Unsupervised Question Decomposition for Question Answering\": https://t.co/iGBdJnRERI\n\nWe decompose a hard Q into several, easier Qs with *unsupervised learning*, improving multi-hop QA on HotpotQA without extra supervision.\n\nw/@PSH_Lewis @scottyih @kchonyc @douwekiela (1/n) https://t.co/Z8ADXlLHoR","live_status":200,"archived_at":"20200225071254","archive":"https://web.archive.org/web/20200225071254/https://twitter.com/EthanJPerez/status/1232127027961942018","via":"wayback","read_at":"2026-09-30"}
